artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let (K, v) be a valued field, with value group vK, residue field Kv, and let K^{ac} denote an algebraic closure of K equipped with the unique extension of v. An algebraic extension (L|K, v) of a henselian valued field (K, v) is called tame if for every finite subextension K'|K of L|K the following three conditions hold:

(T1) the ramification index (vK' : vK) is not divisible by the characteristic of the residue field Kv;
(T2) the residue field extension K'v | Kv is separable;
(T3) the extension (K'|K, v) is defectless, i.e., [K' : K] = (vK' : vK) · [K'v : Kv].

The valued field (K, v) is called a tame field if (K^{ac} | K, v) is a tame extension. It is known that the theory of tame fields is model complete in the language L_val of valued rings, relatively to the theories of the value group vK in the language of ordered abelian groups and of the residue field Kv in the language of rings.

Problem: Determine whether every tame field admits quantifier elimination in some suitable (possibly enriched) language extending L_val together with the natural languages on the value group and residue field sorts. Equivalently, find such a language and prove quantifier elimination, or show that no such enrichment can yield quantifier elimination for the class of tame fields.
